<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest I was Duke of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> from 1806 and, after the Ernestine redistribution of 1826, the first Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>. He gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> Principality of Lichtenberg in 1816 but sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1834. He was the father of Prince Albert, husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha.jpg" alt="Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernst_I,_Duke_of_Saxe-Coburg_and_Gotha_-_Dawe_1818-19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest was born on 2 January 1784, the eldest son of Francis, Duke of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>, and his second wife, Countess Augusta of Reuss-Ebersdorf. His youngest brother Leopold became the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and his sister Victoria was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. When his second son Albert married the queen in 1840, Ernest became both her uncle and her father-in-law. His father fell gravely ill, and Ernest was proclaimed an adult on 10 May 1803 to share in the government. He succeeded as Ernest III in 1806, but French troops occupied the duchy. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Peace of Tilsit</a> in 1807 it was restored to him, through Russian pressure, since his sister Juliane was married to the brother of the Russian tsar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After 1813 Ernest was a Prussian general and fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, then commanded a Prussian corps. Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave him an area of 450 square kilometres with 25,000 inhabitants around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-wendel">St. Wendel</a>, named the Principality of Lichtenberg in 1816. He sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1834.</p><p>When Duke Frederick IV of Saxe-Gotha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> died without an heir in 1825, the Ernestine duchies were redistributed. Ernest was then divorcing his first wife Louise, the late duke's niece, and the other branches used this to bargain against his inheriting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>. In the compromise of 12 November 1826 he received Gotha but ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> to Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, and became Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and Gotha. He had given Coburg a constitution in 1821 and did not interfere in the government of Gotha. In Coburg he built the court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in its new building and shaped the Schlossplatz, and he is remembered for the economic, educational and constitutional development of his lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ernest married Louise of Saxe-Gotha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> in 1817. They had two sons, Ernest II, born in 1818, and Albert, born in 1819. The marriage was unhappy, and they separated in 1824 and were divorced on 31 March 1826; the sons stayed with their father. In 1832 he married his niece Marie of Württemberg, and they had no children. He also had three illegitimate children. He died on 29 January 1844 and was first buried in the Morizkirche, then moved to a new mausoleum in the Friedhof am Glockenberg. Through Albert and his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> gained a significant international position.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest I of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Ernestine redistribution of 1826, Duke <b>Ernest I</b> of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld gave up Saalfeld and received Gotha, forming Saxe-Coburg and Gotha.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Ernest I</b> was restored after French occupation in 1807.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</a></li><li><blockquote>In the redistribution of 1826 Gotha went to the <b>Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</b>, forming the Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha, while Altenburg became the Duchy of Saxe-Altenburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna promised the <b>Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</b> territory for his services against Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lichtenberg">Principality of Lichtenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.26,10.96&amp;year=1823" aria-label="Coburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4801 417 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ernest I, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.26,10.96&amp;year=1823">What was here in 1823? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
